    {
      "autoResize": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "containerId": "rectangle-01100",
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "fontSize": 22,
      "height": 1045,
      "id": "text-01101",
      "index": "bGk",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "originalText": "UX notes\n\n1. Main path is one shareable class link and code, built\non today's enrollment codes (/signup?code=…). Homeschool\nco-ops already talk in a group chat, so Maya can paste\none link there instead of collecting five addresses.\n\n2. Seats is today's \"Max Number Of Users\" field. The\nlink stops working when the class is full or after the\nexpiry date, which limits damage if the link leaks.\n\"Approve each learner\" is the extra guard for a public\nlink.\n\n3. An NDPA cohort can't have an open link: restricted\naccounts must be created by the institution. The link\ncard is replaced by the CSV upload, and the cohort waits\nfor Endless's review.\n\n4. Learner side: the link opens today's sign-up with the\nclass attached. The age gate comes first. Under 13 in\nthe US goes to the Age-restricted page (digital minors\ncan't sign up). 16+ goes straight to the sign-up method.\n\n5. 13–15: the learner gives a parent's email and lands\non the teal \"kids\" consent surface (grey here). Consent\nmoves out of Maya's invite step and into the learner's\nsign-up, so Maya never has to know which families need\nit.\n\n6. Back on the Team Dashboard, the cohort card tracks\nwho's in (Joined, Needs parent consent, Bounced) next to\nthe Cohort Dashboard placeholder (drawn solid, not\ndotted), and Open Workshop 1 takes Maya into the first\nclass.\n\nEmail is still there (\"Send the link by email\") for\nfamilies who aren't in the group chat. Bounced only\napplies to those emails.",
      "roughness": 0,
      "seed": 285100125,
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "text": "UX notes\n\n1. Main path is one shareable class link and code, built\non today's enrollment codes (/signup?code=…). Homeschool\nco-ops already talk in a group chat, so Maya can paste\none link there instead of collecting five addresses.\n\n2. Seats is today's \"Max Number Of Users\" field. The\nlink stops working when the class is full or after the\nexpiry date, which limits damage if the link leaks.\n\"Approve each learner\" is the extra guard for a public\nlink.\n\n3. An NDPA cohort can't have an open link: restricted\naccounts must be created by the institution. The link\ncard is replaced by the CSV upload, and the cohort waits\nfor Endless's review.\n\n4. Learner side: the link opens today's sign-up with the\nclass attached. The age gate comes first. Under 13 in\nthe US goes to the Age-restricted page (digital minors\ncan't sign up). 16+ goes straight to the sign-up method.\n\n5. 13–15: the learner gives a parent's email and lands\non the teal \"kids\" consent surface (grey here). Consent\nmoves out of Maya's invite step and into the learner's\nsign-up, so Maya never has to know which families need\nit.\n\n6. Back on the Team Dashboard, the cohort card tracks\nwho's in (Joined, Needs parent consent, Bounced) next to\nthe Cohort Dashboard placeholder (drawn solid, not\ndotted), and Open Workshop 1 takes Maya into the first\nclass.\n\nEmail is still there (\"Send the link by email\") for\nfamilies who aren't in the group chat. Bounced only\napplies to those emails.",
      "textAlign": "left",
      "type": "text",
      "version": 2,
      "versionNonce": 922997160,
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "width": 736,
      "x": 5974,
      "y": 10103
    }
